  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. I haven't opened the dashboard to check how the panels look. The rule was created enabled but its status was still \"pending\", so it hasn't run yet.\n\n**What the data holds:** `kibana_sample_data_logs` is web-server traffic with these fields:\n- `timestamp`\n- `response` (a string status code)\n- `bytes`\n- `extension`\n- `geo.src` and `geo.dest`\n- `clientip`\n- `machine.os`\n- `url`\n- `agent`\n- `referer`\n\nThe data only has 200, 404 and 503 responses, so every 5xx request is a 503.\n\n**Dashboard \"EFF-aa786aeb Web logs overview\"** has five panels:\n- Total requests\n- Requests over time\n- Responses by status code\n- Bytes by file extension\n- Top source countries\n\nYou can open it at http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-aa786aeb-web-logs-overview-3fa3b2d4e29d.\n\n**Rule \"EFF-aa786aeb 5xx spike\"** (id `957f2050-1010-414e-993e-3537437a57a4`):\n- It checks every minute.\n- It fires when more than 10 documents with a `response` starting with \"5\" arrive in a 5-minute window.\n- It has no notification action attached, so it only records alerts in Kibana.\n\nThe sample data's timestamps run from 2026-10-04 to 2026-12-03, and today is 2026-10-11. The rule only counts documents from the last 5 minutes, so it will only fire if new 5xx documents arrive.",
